{"ts": 1789779372.830354, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Evaluation is deterministic (repeat gives exactly 1.074), and no alternative triple comes close. I'll submit {37, 35, 16}, all long.\n\nNow for the prediction. The key question is how much of that 1.074 is real versus selection noise. My reasoning:\n\n**Why I discount the in-sample number:**\n- I ran ~66 evaluations and picked the max. With n=5000 daily obs (~20 years), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/20) \u2248 0.22. Selecting the best of many correlated candidates inflates the estimate.\n- Crucially, though, the winner here wasn't a fluke of search: features 37, 35, 16 were the top three on *standalone* Sharpe before I looked at any combination. The triple was essentially predetermined by the marginal ranking, not discovered by dredging interactions. That sharply limits the selection bias \u2014 I didn't need to search to find it.\n- The combination gain is also mechanically explicable. Three roughly uncorrelated signals at ~0.61 each should give ~0.61\u00d7\u221a3 \u2248 1.06, and I observe 1.074. There's no anomalous interaction effect being exploited, which is exactly what I'd expect to decay.\n\n**Why I still shade downward:** each component's individual Sharpe is itself a selected quantity (best of 40), so the ~0.61 estimates are somewhat optimistic, and that propagates. The bar of 1.024 is calibrated to this search intensity, and I only clear it by 0.05 \u2014 a signal that my edge over a null-adjusted benchmark is modest.\n\nNetting these: I expect meaningful decay from 1.074, but not collapse, since the structure is honest diversification of independently-strong signals rather than a fitted interaction."}
