{"ts": 1789779418.933641, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features {16, 35, 37}, all long. In-sample Sharpe **1.074** vs. search-adjusted bar of 1.024.\n\n**Method.** Swept all 40 features individually (40 evals), then tested pairs and triples among the top candidates using each feature's best sign (~26 more evals).\n\n**What the data showed.** Individual Sharpes spanned roughly \u00b10.62. The top three long signals \u2014 37 (0.621), 35 (0.614), 16 (0.606) \u2014 turned out to be the winning triple. Pairs combined near-additively in variance, indicating the features are largely mutually uncorrelated. The triple's 1.074 is almost exactly the 0.61\u00d7\u221a3 \u2248 1.06 you'd predict from independent signals, so the gain is plain diversification, not a fitted interaction. Sign direction was confirmed by flipping feature 35, which collapsed the result to 0.349. Repeat evaluation returned an identical value, so the metric is deterministic.\n\n**Prediction: mean 0.85, sd 0.30.**\n\nI shaded down from 1.074 for two reasons. Each component's ~0.61 is itself a max-of-40 statistic and therefore optimistic, and that bias propagates into the combination. I also clear the calibrated bar by only 0.05, which says my edge over a null-adjusted benchmark is modest. Working against heavy discounting: the triple was determined by the *standalone* ranking before I evaluated any combination, so it wasn't found by dredging interactions \u2014 that materially limits selection bias. With ~20 years of data the SE on an annualized Sharpe is about 0.22; I widened to 0.30 to absorb the selection uncertainty I can't fully quantify.\n\nTwo caveats worth flagging. All results are in-sample with no holdout available on my side, so the OOS estimate rests on reasoning about the selection process rather than direct validation. And the sweep assumed each feature's best sign carries over into combinations \u2014 a reasonable assumption given the low correlations, though I verified it directly for only one feature.\n\nSeparately: several claude.ai MCP connectors reported needing authorization. They're irrelevant to this task and I didn't use them, but if you want them available they'd need authorizing via your claude.ai connector settings, or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
